---
/**
 * Landing page. Nimbus has no splash template — landing pages are just Astro
 * pages — so the hero is authored here and the cards reuse the same
 * <Card>/<CardGrid> the docs use.
 */
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import Header from "../components/Header.astro";
import { Card } from "../components/ui/card";
import { CardGrid } from "../components/ui/card-grid";
import { LinkButton } from "../components/ui/link-button";
import { config } from "virtual:nimbus/config";

const tagline =
  "React Server Components as a Vite plugin. Routing, streaming, server actions and build-time prerendering — small enough to read in an afternoon, strict enough that an agent gets it right.";

const preset = `nitro({ preset: 'cloudflare_module' })`;

// What the build says, verbatim, when a page reads the request above every
// boundary. The point of showing it: the refusal names the fix.
const refusal = `  ○  /        82 kB
  ✗  /orders
     reads the request before anything can paint. Add a loading.tsx beside it,
     or put a <Suspense> above the waiting, and it has a skeleton to store.

  1 static, 1 failed`;
---

<BaseLayout title={config.title} description={config.description}>
  <div class="flex flex-col min-h-screen">
    <Header showSidebar={false} />
    <main id="main-content" tabindex="-1" class="flex-1 mx-auto max-w-3xl w-full px-6 py-20">
      <h1
        class="text-foreground leading-tight"
        style="font-size: var(--nb-h1-size); font-weight: var(--nb-h1-weight); letter-spacing: var(--nb-h1-tracking);"
      >
        {config.title}
      </h1>
      <p class="mt-3 text-[1.0625rem] text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
        {tagline}
      </p>

      <pre class="mt-6 rounded-lg bg-card ring-1 ring-border px-4 py-3 overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>bun create rsc-kit@latest my-app</code></pre>

      <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap items-center gap-3">
        <LinkButton href="/quick-start" variant="primary" size="lg" icon>
          Quick start
        </LinkButton>
        {config.github && (
          <LinkButton
            href={config.github}
            variant="secondary"
            size="lg"
            target="_blank"
            rel="noopener"
          >
            View on GitHub
          </LinkButton>
        )}
      </div>

      <h2 class="mt-16 text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">Deploying is one string</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
        There is no server file to write. Nitro builds one around your route tree, so moving
        from a container to Workers to Vercel is a change of preset — not a rewrite, and not an
        adapter package to wait for.
      </p>
      <pre class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-card ring-1 ring-border px-4 py-3 overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>{preset}</code></pre>

      <h2 class="mt-16 text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">Built for you, and for the agent you work with</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
        Most of what goes wrong in a React app compiles fine. So the rules are not in a
        linter — they are in the types, the build and the tests, where they cannot be skipped.
        The build renders every route, refuses what cannot be right, and says what to change:
      </p>
      <pre class="mt-4 rounded-lg bg-card ring-1 ring-border px-4 py-3 overflow-x-auto text-sm"><code>{refusal}</code></pre>
      <p class="mt-4 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
        Every project ships with <code>AGENTS.md</code> for the rules that differ from Next, a
        <code>.mcp.json</code> that connects an <a href="/guides/mcp" class="underline">MCP server</a> answering
        from your last build — which routes froze, why one is dynamic, what a page costs — and
        every guide, offline. Tests go <a href="/guides/testing" class="underline">through the real
        build</a>, and <code>bun run check</code> is the one command to run before calling it done.
        These docs are markdown too: add <code>index.md</code> to any url, or start at
        <a href="/llms.txt" class="underline"><code>/llms.txt</code></a>.
      </p>

      <h2 class="mt-16 text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">Why this one</h2>
      <CardGrid class="mt-4">
        <Card title="Deploys anywhere" icon="ph:plugs-connected">
          Bun, Node, Cloudflare Workers, Vercel, Netlify, Deno — every target Nitro carries,
          none of them an adapter this project has to write and keep working.
        </Card>
        <Card title="Instant navigation" icon="ph:lightning">
          Links prefetch on hover and replace only the part of the page that changed. Scroll
          position, open menus and a half-typed form all survive.
        </Card>
        <Card title="Fast by default" icon="ph:gauge">
          Pages that need nothing dynamic are rendered at build time, automatically. The rest
          get a prerendered shell with their data streamed in. Nothing to configure.
        </Card>
        <Card title="Lightweight" icon="ph:feather">
          <strong class="text-foreground">6 kB</strong> gzipped on top of React — 7% of the
          JavaScript a starter app ships. A page with nothing interactive can ship none at all.
        </Card>
        <Card title="Keeps your Vite setup" icon="ph:plug">
          Tailwind, PostCSS and any Vite plugin work the way they do in any Vite app. There is
          no bundler of ours to configure around.
        </Card>
        <Card title="API routes, no config" icon="ph:plugs-connected">
          A <code>route.ts</code> beside your pages answers a url. Export <code>GET</code> or <code>POST</code>, take a
          Request, return a Response.
        </Card>
        <Card title="An agent gets it right" icon="ph:robot">
          The build refuses what cannot work and names the fix. An MCP server reads that
          report, and the guides ship with it. What compiles but is wrong has nowhere to hide.
        </Card>
        <Card title="Tests through the real app" icon="ph:check-circle">
          <code>createTestApp()</code> hands back the deployed handler — no port, no browser.
          Actions, queries and api routes are plain functions you can call.
        </Card>
      </CardGrid>

      <h2 class="mt-16 text-xl font-semibold text-foreground">What ships to the browser</h2>
      <p class="mt-2 text-muted-foreground leading-relaxed">
        <strong class="text-foreground">82 kB</strong> of gzipped JavaScript on the starter app,
        and <strong class="text-foreground">66 kB</strong> of that is React itself. rsc-kit's own
        runtime is <strong class="text-foreground">6 kB</strong>; the rest is React's RSC client.
        A route with nothing interactive on it can ship <a href="/guides/no-javascript" class="underline">no JavaScript at all</a>.
      </p>
    </main>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
